@* The header of the one card for list pages (EntityCard): the avatar, the
   title and the subtitle, and a badge on the right. Its own component so the
   card of a resource on the dashboard wears the same header as the list's card,
   drawn once. While a verb runs on the row
   (BusyRows, by Key), or while it is on its way (the Avatar a page hands
   over), the avatar is a ring: everything under way is a ring, and the
   avatar's own place costs the card no height. A page may give the header a
   tone of the palette that says the row's state at a glance (a running
   container's is green); without one it keeps the card's own. *@
@implements IDisposable
@inject BusyRows Rows

<MudCardHeader Style="@CardTone.Style(Tone)">
    <CardHeaderAvatar>
        @* A row on its way puts its progress where the avatar is: the card says
           at a glance what it is doing, in the place the eye already goes to.
           Busy first: a page hands its Avatar fragment for every row (a pull or a
           run on its way draws its ring there, the rest their letter), so a verb
           running on the row has to win over it or it would never show. *@
        @if (Rows.IsBusy(Key))
        {
            @* A verb running on this row (start, stop, restart, remove): the
               ring turns where the avatar is, as a pull's does. There is no
               percentage to tell — wslc reports none — so it just turns. *@
            <div class="wslc-progress-ring" title="Working…">
                <MudProgressCircular Class="wslc-progress-ring-dial" Color="Color.Primary" Indeterminate="true" />
            </div>
        }
        else if (Avatar is not null)
        {
            @Avatar
        }
        else
        {
            <MudAvatar Color="AvatarColor" Variant="Variant.Filled">@Initial</MudAvatar>
        }
    </CardHeaderAvatar>
    <CardHeaderContent>
        @* Titles are names the user chose — a volume's, an image's — and they
           can be longer than the card. They are cut here, with the whole of
           them in the tooltip, because a name that runs past the card's edge
           lands on the card beside it. *@
        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="wslc-card-line" title="@Title">@Title</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mud-text-secondary wslc-card-line" title="@Subtitle">@Subtitle</MudText>
    </CardHeaderContent>
    <CardHeaderActions>
        @HeaderActions
    </CardHeaderActions>
</MudCardHeader>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = "";

    [Parameter] public string Subtitle { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>Avatar letter; defaults to the first character of the title.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? AvatarText { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public Color AvatarColor { get; set; } = Color.Primary;

    /// <summary>Drawn instead of the avatar: a row on its way shows its progress there.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Avatar { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The row's key, the one its actions component reports busy with.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Key { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? HeaderActions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The header's background saying the row's state (<see cref="CardTone"/>); null for the card's own tone.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Color? Tone { get; set; }

    private string Initial => string.IsNullOrEmpty(AvatarText)
        ? (Title.Length > 0 ? Title[..1].ToUpperInvariant() : "?")
        : AvatarText;

    protected override void OnInitialized() => Rows.Changed += OnChanged;

    private void OnChanged() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public void Dispose() => Rows.Changed -= OnChanged;
}
